WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.310
- So have you met Chloe?

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.940
- I've never met both of you.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:04.773
- [Kelly] Wait, what?

00:00:04.773 --> 00:00:05.770
You never met Drax?

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:06.603
- I know, I know.

00:00:06.603 --> 00:00:08.306
- How - you're married to Chris Pratt.

00:00:08.306 --> 00:00:10.214
- I know, it's very bizarre.

00:00:10.214 --> 00:00:11.047
- How are you?

00:00:11.047 --> 00:00:12.060
- Hi, nice to meet you.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.740
- Well, I'm just bringing people together.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.240
- I know, it was so funny
cause I told my husband

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.073
I was coming on this show

00:00:18.073 --> 00:00:19.350
and he's like, "Oh my
god, that's so amazing."

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.660
I was like, I know I've
heard so much about you,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.350
but I have never met you so.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:24.183
- [Kelly] That's hilarious.

00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:25.100
- Thrilled to finally meet you.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:25.970
- Yeah, likewise.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.320
- Oh my gosh, I love those movies:

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.153
The Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:00:29.153 --> 00:00:31.330
Okay, anyway, I'm fine, I'm fine.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.310
So your husband though,
he just, Chris Pratt,

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:34.750
he had a birthday, right?

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.560
A recent birthday, what'd you get him?

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.200
- I got him piggies.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.670
- [Kelly] You got him actual pigs?

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:40.510
- Yeah, I did.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:41.930
- What, like miniature ones?

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.080
- No, no, well actually
what happened to me

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.580
when I was in high school was I rescued

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.690
this supposed miniature pig

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.850
when I lived at my parents house,

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.460
and it turned into a giant potbelly pig

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.290
and rooted up my mom's entire lawn.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.123
- I'm just laughing because

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.610
if nobody's actually had a huge
potbelly, they're enormous.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.640
- They're so big, very, very large.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.890
Yeah that's Bacon, my pig,
she's since passed away.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:05.820
- You named her bacon.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.640
- I know, my little brother Christopher

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.010
actually named her
Bacon so it's his fault.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.730
But yeah, he has, we have an amazing place

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:14.660
in Washington state so
there's a lot of land there,

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.330
so these pigs are not living with us,

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:18.376
they're living in like
a very beautiful farm.

00:01:18.376 --> 00:01:20.340
- [Kelly] On a farm, yeah.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.170
- Loving life, very large
now so I'm very excited

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.380
that they did not come to our house

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.930
because I was told they would stay small.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:26.870
They did not.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:29.180
- Yeah, I was told my dog
was gonna be miniature

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.643
and lies and deception.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.610
Heffer's like forty-five pounds.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.360
I'm like what, he is not
fifteen to eighteen pounds.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.200
That's awesomeness, y'all
have a farm in Washington?

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.280
Washington's beautiful.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:40.168
- Yeah, yeah it's very nice.

00:01:40.168 --> 00:01:41.001
Yeah, yeah, yeah, beautiful.

00:01:41.001 --> 00:01:42.330
- [Kelly] Nice place
to escape, that's cool.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.620
- Yeah, I grew up around
animals so it's really fun.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:45.510
- [Kelly] I did as well.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.520
- I love it, It makes me so happy.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.810
- Animals are so cool.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.486
They're just loyal and nice
and they're never mean.

00:01:50.486 --> 00:01:53.306
So have y'all ever had any unusual pets?

00:01:53.306 --> 00:01:56.330
- I used to have this babysitter
and she had a rooster.

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.250
- Oh, they can be mean.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.210
- [Katherine] Yeah, and loud.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.020
- That's the thing.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.550
It was very mean cause
she would go outside

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:04.610
and she loved the rooster

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.130
because how can you not love animals?

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.909
And she would go outside
and try to bond with it,

00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:09.742
and it would try to bite her.

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:12.450
And at a certain point,

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:14.350
it was chasing her all around the yard,

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:15.420
and she climbed up a tree,

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.830
and it bit her butt, and it was horrible.

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:19.030
- Oh my god, that's what happened to me.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.700
One time at a little
petting zoo, my sister,

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:21.533
I was holding her child,

00:02:21.533 --> 00:02:23.150
and this goat kept nipping at my behind.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.073
I was like, "Get your child off of me!"

00:02:25.073 --> 00:02:27.390
Cause I was holding her
kid like from the goat

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:28.890
and like, my sister is just laughing.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.501
I'm like, "It's eating my butt!"

00:02:30.501 --> 00:02:31.850
I was like, "Quick!"

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.140
Anyway, no I love random pets though,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.380
I love, I love all kinds
of pets, especially farms.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.780
It's nice to get out
in like the wilderness,

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:39.613
where they're out of the city.

00:02:39.613 --> 00:02:41.349
- I like having them in like a
home in the city though, too.

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:42.200
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.160
- I also grew up like that.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.430
My dad has a miniature
pony and a donkey currently

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.680
that he brings in the house constantly.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:48.720
- [Kelly] In your house?

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.790
- In his house, yes.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.760
- Oh good, correction, in his house.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.390
You can do whatever
you want in your house.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.223
- Exactly.

00:02:55.223 --> 00:02:56.270
- [Kelly] Okay, well let's
talk about your book.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:59.220
So The Gift of Forgiveness,
what inspired you to do this?

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.450
- Really just life.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.940
I think I, you know,
felt as many of us do,

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.110
like I was struggling with
forgiveness in my own life

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:09.130
and I went to try and
find help and resources

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:11.610
and I couldn't find anything
that really spoke to me.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.800
And I felt that speaking to other people

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.710
about their journeys and their struggles

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.190
is really what inspired me
to be able to go to my life

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:19.170
and practice forgiveness.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.240
So I decided to create that.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.360
This book has a series
of incredible stories

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.500
that are just really mind
blowing and these people

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.390
have all been able to talk
about practicing forgiveness

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:32.630
and struggling with forgiveness

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.100
and some still struggling with it,

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.063
so it's a really awesome story.

00:03:36.063 --> 00:03:38.190
- I think it's amazing
cause even though sometimes

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:41.010
in my life I feel like I
have forgiven a situation,

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:41.843
then you're like,

00:03:41.843 --> 00:03:43.077
"Oh, this isn't just
like a one time thing,

00:03:43.077 --> 00:03:45.290
"I have to keep practicing at this."

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.770
Like, because I don't
think I did fully forgive.

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:49.100
- Yeah, and it was actually
something interesting

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.810
that was in the book and also interesting

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:51.643
cause people are always like,

00:03:51.643 --> 00:03:53.277
"Okay, so what's the quick fix

00:03:53.277 --> 00:03:54.990
"on how do I practice forgiveness?"

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.020
And I keep reminding people that

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:58.990
what forgiveness means to
you is something totally

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:00.730
than what it means to me which makes it

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.960
so much more complicated
but also really interesting

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:06.270
because for a lot of us,
it's a one and done thing

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.490
which in my book, there are some people

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.150
who practice forgiveness and
they're done and they're good.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.100
And then there are a
lot of us, like myself,

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.050
who it's an ongoing process

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:16.610
and so what I like to say to people

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.010
is that I have struggled with forgiveness

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.250
and I've been able to practice it

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.200
but I'm a fellow
struggler with forgiveness

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.070
and it's an ongoing process.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.900
But when I struggle, I'm
able to now draw inspiration

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.310
from the stories in my book
to kind of help get me through

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.780
and that's been incredibly helpful for me.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.129
- Yeah, because some of
the people you interviewed,

00:04:32.129 --> 00:04:34.390
that's a hefty amount of forgiving.

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:38.150
- I know, these names are like
Elizabeth Smart, Sue Klebold,

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.690
Pablo Escobar's son, Sebastián Marroquín,

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:45.690
and we have so many incredible,
amazing people in the book

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.120
and just a really wide range of stories

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.330
which was really important to me,

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.990
people from all different
backgrounds, all walks of life,

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.150
all different faiths, some with no faith.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:57.240
So, it was important to me to
get a good mixture of people

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.671
who've experienced a variety of different,

00:04:58.671 --> 00:04:59.504
- [Kelly] It's inclusive.

00:04:59.504 --> 00:05:02.690
- Yeah, and so somebody can
read it and relate to something

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.180
and also, I encourage
people to kind of pick it up

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.060
and put it down because it is a lot.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:07.893
- [Kelly] A heavy read?

00:05:07.893 --> 00:05:10.610
- Yeah, it's a heavy read but
it's also really inspiring

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.360
because people gave me the gift

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.910
of just really being so open

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.820
and honest and raw and vulnerable with me

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:17.250
which was incredible

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.600
and now with anybody
who gets to read it, so.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.700
- Have y'all, do you feel
like you've ever had dig deep

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.850
to like forgive someone, I have.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.979
- I'm not really great at forgiving.

00:05:26.979 --> 00:05:28.404
I probably need to read the book.

00:05:28.404 --> 00:05:29.237
- [Kelly] I probably need
to read the book (laughs).

00:05:29.237 --> 00:05:30.250
- I'll have him over for dinner

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.283
and just talk about it some more.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:37.040
- Hey, you're eleven but hey,
a lot of people count kids out

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:39.740
but a lot of things
happen when you're a kid

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.110
that can be devastating.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:42.570
Like, I mean my girl's five years old

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:43.860
and she has had her heart broken already

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.839
just by friendships, you know?

00:05:45.839 --> 00:05:46.672
- Exactly!

00:05:46.672 --> 00:05:48.930
- Yeah, all the sudden,
some girl's just mean to her

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:49.763
out of no where and she's like,

00:05:49.763 --> 00:05:50.640
"Wait, I thought we were friends."

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:51.473
So you know, that's pretty devastating.

00:05:51.473 --> 00:05:55.560
- I know, it is like a,
especially this generation,

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.140
I feel like too many
kids are worried about

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.070
how other people see them instead of

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:00.903
how they see their self.

00:06:00.903 --> 00:06:02.478
- It's a daily conversation
I have with my daughter.

00:06:02.478 --> 00:06:05.400
- And they feel like they have
to fit in and match everybody

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.870
and it's just like that's how it is.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.850
Like, either you're in
the group or you're out

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.140
and it's like, it shouldn't be that way,

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.340
you should just accept
everybody for you know,

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.430
how they are and that's
how you have real friends,

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:17.467
if you're the real you, yeah.

